   130	\subsection*{Governing-law structures}
   131	
   132	\begin{definition}[Paramagnetic torus laws along a path]
   133	\label{def:IPhO2026Problems_problem_IPhO_2026_3_B_2:ParamagneticTorusLaws}
   134	\lean{IPhO2026_3_B_2.ParamagneticTorusLaws}
   135	\uses{def:IPhO2026Problems_problem_IPhO_2026_3_B_2:TorusParameters, def:IPhO2026Problems_problem_IPhO_2026_3_B_2:StatePath}
   136	The physical laws of the problem, bundled along a quasistatic path: positive
   137	temperature at every point (regularity needed for $C_M = n\lambda/T^2$ and
   138	division by $T$); the equation of state $T M V = n K H$; a heat-capacity
   139	function $C_M(t) = n\lambda/T(t)^2$, integrable on every interval; and the
   140	magnetic-work rate $\mu_0 V H(t)\,dM/dt$ of part A.3, likewise integrable.
   141	The integrability clauses bundle the smoothness of the quasistatic process
   142	needed to integrate the differential forms.
   143	\textbf{Path regularity (iter-012 repair R1).}  The bundle must additionally
   144	require that the quasistatic path itself supplies the differentiability the
   145	work/first-law identities differentiate: the redrafted Lean fields
   146	\texttt{temp\_differentiable} and \texttt{mag\_differentiable} assert
   147	$\forall t$, \texttt{DifferentiableAt $\mathbb{R}$} of
   148	$t \mapsto T(t)$ and $t \mapsto M(t)$ at every parameter $t$ (field
   149	regularity follows: $H(t) = T(t)M(t)V/(nK)$ is a differentiable combination
   150	because $n, K > 0$).  Without these fields $\mathrm{d}/\mathrm{d}t$ is junk
   151	($= 0$) off the differentiable locus, the first-law balance degenerates to
   152	$0 = 0$, and a path with arbitrary jumps between two parameters satisfies
   153	every law while the invariant differs at the endpoints --- the formalization
   154	Review's countermodel class.  Quasistatic adiabatic ramps are by definition
   155	smooth, so requiring pointwise differentiability is faithful to the process,
   156	not an extra physical assumption.
   157	\end{definition}
   158	\begin{proof}
   159	Definition; no claim.
   160	\end{proof}
   161	
   162	\begin{definition}[Adiabatic process law]
   163	\label{def:IPhO2026Problems_problem_IPhO_2026_3_B_2:IsAdiabaticPath}
   164	\lean{IPhO2026_3_B_2.IsAdiabaticPath}
   165	\uses{def:IPhO2026Problems_problem_IPhO_2026_3_B_2:ParamagneticTorusLaws}
   166	Along an adiabatic path no heat enters the torus, so the first law
   167	$dU = \delta Q + dW$ with the stated sign convention and $dU = C_M\,dT$
   168	forces the heat-capacity term to balance the magnetic work rate pointwise
   169	along the path: $C_M(t)\,dT/dt = -w(t)$.  This is the physical first law
   170	for zero heat transfer, not the final temperature formula.
   171	With the path-differentiability fields of
   172	\cref{def:IPhO2026Problems_problem_IPhO_2026_3_B_2:ParamagneticTorusLaws}
   173	in force, every $\mathrm{d}/\mathrm{d}t$ in the balance is the value of an
   174	actual derivative, so the balance is informative at every parameter.
   175	\end{definition}
   176	\begin{proof}
   177	Definition; no claim.
   178	\end{proof}
